## Multi-agent workflow
<!-- This section and "Model policy" below are the reusable part of this
     template — copy them as-is into every project's CLAUDE.md. They
     describe the coordinator in .claude/commands/code-task.md and the
     two subagents in .claude/agents/. -->

- Write a task in `Artifacts/TASK.md` (just `# Task` and `# Notes` — no
  difficulty field in this setup) and run `/code-task`.
- Only the coordinator (the `/code-task` command, run in the main session —
  not a subagent) writes `Artifacts/state.json`.
- The coordinator itself appends to `Artifacts/dev-diary.md` once a run
  finishes (passed, or done-unverified if `CLAUDE.md` has no check
  commands) — there is no separate tester agent to do it.
- Agent definitions live in `.claude/agents/` (`planner`, `coder`) — exactly
  two, nothing else; the coordinator lives in
  `.claude/commands/code-task.md`.
- Route (fixed, no difficulty routing): **planner → coder → verify**. The
  planner investigates the codebase itself and writes the plan; the coder
  implements it; verification (test/lint/build) is run by the coordinator
  directly, or by the coder as part of implementing — never a third agent.
- After 2 failed verification runs in a row, the coordinator stops and asks
  you instead of retrying again.
- If an agent dies because the **session/usage limit was hit**, that is a
  pause, not a failure: the coordinator records `status: "paused_rate_limit"`
  in `Artifacts/state.json`, leaves `consecutive_failures` untouched, reports
  what already landed on disk, and stops. Say "continue" once the limit
  resets — it picks up from the interrupted step rather than restarting, and
  never downgrades the model to get around the limit.

### Model policy (a constraint, not a default)
Each agent has exactly one fixed model. It never changes per-call, per
retry, or because a task "looks hard."

| agent | model |
|---|---|
| `planner` | **`opus`** — always |
| `coder` | **`sonnet`** — always |

- **The planner always runs on Opus.** It investigates the codebase itself
  (there is no analyzer) and must produce a plan detailed enough that a
  cheaper model can implement it literally — exact files, line numbers,
  values and call shapes, not "configure it appropriately."
- **The coder always runs on Sonnet** — not on a retry, not when a task
  looks hard. If the coder struggles, the plan wasn't detailed enough: go
  back to the planner, don't upgrade the coder's model. The
  two-consecutive-failure stop above is the backstop, not a model upgrade.
- All agents and the coordinator treat this file as the source of truth for
  commands and conventions — they do not duplicate them elsewhere.
